
# quic-lite

[![CI](https://github.com/llpaca/quic-lite/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/llpaca/quic-lite/actions/workflows/ci.yml)
[![CodeQL](https://github.com/llpaca/quic-lite/actions/workflows/codeql.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/llpaca/quic-lite/actions/workflows/codeql.yml)
[![Language](https://img.shields.io/badge/language-C11-blue.svg)](https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/11.html)
[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](LICENSE)

**quic-lite** is a single-header C implementation of the QUIC v1 transport protocol, built around UDP and [quictls](https://github.com/quictls/openssl) for TLS 1.3 integration.

The project focuses on implementing the core mechanisms behind QUIC, including packet encoding and protection, encrypted handshakes, multiplexed streams, flow control, and loss recovery.

> **Status:** Work in progress. This is an experimental implementation intended for systems programming, protocol development, and learning. It has known protocol gaps and should not be treated as production-ready or fully RFC-compliant.

## Features

### Protocol and packet handling

- QUIC v1 packet structures and header encoding/decoding.
- Variable-length integer encoding and decoding.
- Connection IDs and packet number spaces.
- Frame encoding and decoding, including STREAM, CRYPTO, ACK, PADDING, PING, and connection-control frames.
- Transport parameter encoding and decoding.
- Packet number reconstruction and header protection.

### Cryptography and TLS

- TLS 1.3 handshake integration using quictls.
- Initial, Handshake, and 1-RTT packet protection.
- AEAD encryption and authentication.
- QUIC header protection.
- TLS traffic-secret and packet-key derivation.
- Application traffic key updates.

### Streams and flow control

- Bidirectional and unidirectional streams.
- Stream send and receive buffers.
- Out-of-order stream-data reassembly.
- Stream FIN and RESET_STREAM handling.
- Stream-level and connection-level flow control.
- MAX_DATA, MAX_STREAM_DATA, and stream-limit management.

### Loss recovery and congestion control

- ACK range tracking and acknowledgment processing.
- RTT estimation and packet loss detection.
- Probe Timeout (PTO) handling.
- In-flight packet accounting and retransmission of supported data.
- NewReno-style congestion control.
- Idle timeout and connection closing/draining states.

### Connection management

- Client-side connection establishment.
- Server-side UDP listener and connection demultiplexing.
- Connection ID issuance and retirement.
- Stateless reset generation.
- Path validation and connection migration mechanisms.
- Configurable connection callbacks.

## Prerequisites

The project targets Linux and requires:

- GCC or a compatible C compiler.
- GNU Make.
- Git, Perl, and standard build tools.
- A compatible quictls build exposing the QUIC-specific TLS APIs.
- OpenSSL-compatible headers and libraries supplied by that quictls installation.

The current build instructions use GCC with C11 and POSIX APIs.

### Install build dependencies

**Arch Linux**

```bash
sudo pacman -S --needed git base-devel perl
```

**Ubuntu / Debian**

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y git build-essential perl
```

### Build and install quictls

The following commands build the `openssl-3.1.4-quic1` reference used by the project.

Run these commands from your project directory:

```bash
git clone --branch openssl-3.1.4-quic1 --depth 1 \
    https://github.com/quictls/openssl.git quictls-src

cd quictls-src

./config --prefix="$HOME/quictls-install" --libdir=lib64

make -j"$(nproc)"
make install_sw

cd -
```

This installs quictls under:

```text
$HOME/quictls-install/
├── bin/
├── include/
└── lib64/
```

Verify the installation:

```bash
"$HOME/quictls-install/bin/openssl" version
ls "$HOME/quictls-install/lib64"
```

**Note:** The quictls reference is pinned to a specific branch name. If you change the quictls version, verify compatibility with the QUIC TLS APIs used by `qlite.h`.

## Build

Include `qlite.h` in your application and link against the quictls libraries.

For example, if your project contains:

```text
quic-lite/
├── qlite.h
├── main.c
└── Makefile
```

### Makefile

```makefile
CC = gcc

CFLAGS = -std=c11 -D_POSIX_C_SOURCE=200809L \
         -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror -g \
         -fsanitize=address,undefined \
         -I. -I$(HOME)/quictls-install/include

LDFLAGS = -L$(HOME)/quictls-install/lib64 \
          -Wl,-rpath,$(HOME)/quictls-install/lib64

LDLIBS = -lssl -lcrypto

main: main.c qlite.h
	$(CC) $(CFLAGS) main.c -o main $(LDFLAGS) $(LDLIBS)

clean:
	rm -f main

.PHONY: clean
```

Build and run:

```bash
make
./main
```

Clean the build:

```bash
make clean
```

AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer are enabled for development. Remove `-fsanitize=address,undefined` when building without sanitizers.

**Single-header integration:** The current header contains function definitions as well as type definitions and does not use a separate implementation macro. Include it in only one translation unit per executable to avoid duplicate symbol definitions.

### Compile directly

You can also compile without a Makefile:

```bash
gcc -std=c11 -D_POSIX_C_SOURCE=200809L \
    -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror -g \
    -fsanitize=address,undefined \
    -I. -I"$HOME/quictls-install/include" \
    main.c -o main \
    -L"$HOME/quictls-install/lib64" \
    -Wl,-rpath,"$HOME/quictls-install/lib64" \
    -lssl -lcrypto
```

## API overview

The public API is defined in `qlite.h`.

| Function | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `ql_conn_init()` | Initialize a connection and its protocol state. |
| `qlite_connect()` | Initialize a client connection to a remote IPv4 endpoint. |
| `ql_conn_tick()` | Process network input, advance TLS and protocol state, and flush outgoing datagrams. |
| `qlite_stream_open()` | Open a bidirectional or unidirectional stream. |
| `qlite_send()` | Buffer application data for transmission on a stream. |
| `qlite_recv()` | Read available stream data. |
| `qlite_stream_close()` | Gracefully finish or reset a stream. |
| `qlite_key_update()` | Request an application traffic-key update. |
| `qlite_close()` | Initiate connection shutdown. |
| `qlite_listen()` | Create a server UDP listener. |
| `qlite_listener_tick()` | Receive and dispatch datagrams and advance server connections. |
| `qlite_accept()` | Retrieve a connection whose handshake has completed. |
| `ql_conn_free()` | Release connection-owned resources. |

The TLS context is supplied by the application. Configure its TLS version, certificate and private key where applicable, peer-verification policy, and application protocol negotiation before passing it to the library.

The application is responsible for driving the connection or listener tick functions from its event loop and handling stream and connection callbacks.

Consult the function definitions and associated comments in `qlite.h` for argument requirements and return values.

## Current limitations

This project is under active development. The current implementation has known limitations, including:

- **Address validation:** Retry-based address validation is not implemented in the listener, even when `require_address_validation` is configured.
- **Loss recovery:** PTO probing is simplified compared with the full behavior described in RFC 9002.
- **Retransmission:** Some control-frame retransmission cases, including RESET_STREAM after loss, are simplified or incomplete.
- **Client networking:** The client connection bootstrap currently supports IPv4 only.
- **Interoperability:** Full compliance with all mandatory QUIC behaviors and interoperability with independent QUIC implementations have not been established.

Other features may have additional restrictions. The presence of a frame encoder or decoder does not necessarily mean every corresponding protocol behavior is fully implemented.

## Documentation

The following IETF RFCs are the primary protocol references for this project.

- **[RFC 9000 — QUIC: A UDP-Based Multiplexed and Secure Transport](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9000.html)**  
  Packet formats, variable-length integers, frame encoding, transport parameters, connection IDs, streams, flow control, and connection management.

- **[RFC 9001 — Using TLS to Secure QUIC](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9001.html)**  
  TLS 1.3 integration, CRYPTO frames, packet protection, key derivation, encryption levels, and key updates.

- **[RFC 9002 — QUIC Loss Detection and Congestion Control](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9002.html)**  
  ACK processing, RTT estimation, loss detection, PTO, bytes in flight, and congestion control.

- **[RFC 8999 — Version-Independent Properties of QUIC](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8999.html)**  
  Version-independent packet properties and invariants.

For additional specifications and extensions, see the [IETF QUIC working group](https://datatracker.ietf.org/group/quic/documents/).

## Security

This project implements security-sensitive transport and cryptographic logic. It is experimental and has not been established as suitable for production deployment.

Do not rely on it to protect sensitive traffic in production environments. Security testing, interoperability testing, fuzzing, and review against the relevant RFC requirements are important before considering broader use.

To report a vulnerability, avoid publishing exploit details in a public issue until a responsible disclosure process has been established.

## Contributing

Contributions are welcome, particularly in the following areas:

- Protocol correctness and RFC conformance.
- Parser robustness and malformed-packet handling.
- Fuzz testing and sanitizer coverage.
- Loss recovery and congestion-control improvements.
- Interoperability testing against established QUIC implementations.
- Documentation and reproducible test cases.

When submitting a change, include a clear description of the behavior being changed and tests demonstrating the expected result.

